\subsection{Introduction}

The course aims to give an introduction to the use of \textbf{HDL}s (\textit{Hardware Description Languages}):
\begin{itemize}
	\item
	      We will see HDLs used as \textit{specification languages} used to design hardware.
	      Both \textit{Verilog} and \textit{VHDL} are used, respectively industry and academic based;

	\item
	      High level concepts will be studied at the \textbf{RT} (\textit{Register Transfer}) level of abstraction. We'll also see design methodologies for \textbf{ASIC}s (\textit{Application-Specific Integrated Circuits}) and \textbf{FPGA}s (\textit{Field Programmable Gate Arrays});

	\item
	      Simulation tools are the main tool available for verification and validation of designs. Other important tools include \textbf{CAD}-based formal verification, and mathematical \textit{formal verification} methods.
\end{itemize}

The topics covered will be:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Usage of \textbf{simulation} tools such as the Xilinx Vivado suite of programs;
	\item \textbf{Combinatorial} networks, that is networks where the output value is only determined by the values taken by their inputs;
	\item \textbf{Sequential} networks, that is networks where the output value is determined both by the values taken by the inputs and internal state. Sequential networns can either be \textit{asynchronous} or \textit{synchronized} to an external clock signal;
	\item \textbf{FSM}s (\textit{Finite State Machines}), which we will see as a specific class of sequential circuits;
	\item \textbf{FSM-D} models (FSMs operating at logical level, controlling \textit{datapaths} for data manipulation);
	\item \textbf{RAM}s (\textit{Random Access Memories}) and \textbf{ROM}s (\textit{Read-Only Memories}), and their respective interfaces facing other components (including ones we will design ourselves).
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{VHDL as a specification language}

VHDL will be used and explained in a design-oriented approach covering:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Combinatorial} logic;
	\item \textit{Synchronous} \textbf{sequential} logic (with \textit{asynchronous} sequential logic being mostly a less useful superset);
	\item \textbf{Datapath} components.
\end{enumerate}
Verilog will be covered, by \textit{difference} from VHDL.
Still, both languages are useful in academic and industrial contexts, and proficiency in both is expected in most design environments.

\subsubsection{Register Transfer level designs}

We will then move upwards to the \textbf{RT} (\textit{Register Transfer}) level of abstraction.
RT level is a design abstraction which models a synchronous digital circuit in terms of the flow of digital signals (data) between hardware registers, and the logical operations performed on those signals.

Using this abstraction we will design embedded systems, covering design \textit{metrics} (related to economical, manifacturing, ... factors), design \textit{methodologies} for \textbf{single-purpose} processors.

By \textit{single-purpose}, we mean processors that implement a specific algorithm in hardware (ASICs, FPGAs): this is the opposite concept to \textit{general-purpose} computing.

\textbf{FSM} (\textit{Finite State Machine}) optimization will also be covered.

\subsubsection{Formal verification}

The main tool used to verify a design is \textbf{simulation}, and simulation through the Xilinx suite of programs will be studied.

Another method that will be studied is \textbf{formal verification}, that is mathematical methods used to prove the validity of designs indipendent of the given inputs:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textit{Theorem proving} involves expressing the hardware and desired property mathematically, then proving the property from axioms/rules;
	\item \textit{Equivalence checking} entails, given two designs, proving that they behave equivalently under specified conditions;
	\item \textit{Model checking} means determining if certain \textit{properties} of our designed system can be proved to be true indipendently of the inputs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{VHDL for digital design}

A \textbf{digital system} is an electronic system operating on data encoded as a sequence of zeroes and ones (low and high, off or on values).

When digital design started (aruond the 1960s), the number of components in a circuit was very limited.
As of today, that number as grown up to billions of components on a single chip.

This growth has made the \textit{documentation} process harder and harder, and the resulting artifacts more and more imprecise and ambiguous.

\subsubsection{HDLs}

The main problem is that \textit{natural language} is not fit for description of a digital system.
For this reason, machine-readable textual languages were invented for describing hardware.
We call these languages \textbf{HDL}s (\textit{Hardware Description Languages}).

HDLs are the opposite of \textit{programming} languages: they don't describe \textit{how} an algorithm is implemented, can't be executed, but describe the \textit{temporal behavoir} and \textit{spatial structure} of an hardware system.
This allows to combine design and documentation in a single source of truth (documentation of a system can be extracted from its HDL description).

An HDL usually allows us to define:
\begin{itemize}
	\item A \textbf{black box} representation of a given system, which only entails its inputs and outputs (which we call the \textit{interface} of the system);
	\item An \textbf{architectural} view of the system, which entails how the inputs (and optionally, previous state) are combined to generate the outputs.
	      The architecture of a system can be defined in terms of other systems (such as \textit{And}, \textit{Or} and \textit{Not} gates), themselves taken as other black boxes with their own architectural implementation.
\end{itemize}

Of course, the set of components used to make up the architectural description of a system represents its \textit{spatial structure}.
The \textit{temporal behavoir}, on the other hand, is specified using other language constructs that specify the timing constants between components.

\subsubsection{VHDL}

\textbf{VHDL} (\textit{VHSIC Hardware Description Language}), with \textbf{VHSIC} (\textit{Very High Speed Integrated Circuit}) being a project of the U.S. Dept. of Defense, is a hardware specification language based on a top-bottom approach.
The syntax is similar to \textit{Ada}, another U.S. Dept. of Defense creation.
Today it's mainly used in academia, and is better suited to high-level behavioral modeling.

\subsubsection{Verilog}

\textbf{Verilog} was defined in 1995 at \textit{Gateway Design Automation Inc.}, then acquired by \textit{Cadence Design Systems}.
The initial implementation was proprietary, and meant for designers to quickly iterate over prototypes in a bottom-up approach.
Afterwards, the language was extended and made into an open standard.
The syntax is C-like, and today it's the most significant difference from VHDL.
Another difference is that Verilog is more fit for low-level work than VHDL.

\subsubsection{Comparison of VHDL and Verilog}

We said that Verilog will be studied by difference.

Other differences to note are noted in the table:
\begin{table}[H]
	\center \rowcolors{2}{white}{black!10}
	\begin{tabular} { p{7cm} | p{7cm} }
		\bfseries VHDL                                                                                       & \bfseries Verilog                 \\
		\hline
		Ada-like, not \textit{case sensitive}                                                                &
		C-like, \textit{case sensitive}                                                                                                          \\
		Strongly typed, compile time error checking                                                          &
		Weakly typed, less compile time checking, more likely runtime bugs                                                                       \\
		Focused on safety                                                                                    & Focused on writing models quickly \\
		User-defined types, 9 value logic                                                                    &
		No user-defined types, 4 value logic                                                                                                     \\
		\lstset{language=VHDL}
		Hierarchy through \lstinline|entity| definitions and implementation through \lstinline|architecture| &
		\lstset{language=Verilog}
		Hierarchy through \lstinline|module| definitions with self contained implementation                                                      \\
		Gate level modeling with \textbf{FPGA} library support                                               &
		Better \textbf{ASIC} support, user defined primitives                                                                                    \\
		Assertions                                                                                           & No assertions                     \\
		No named events                                                                                      & Named events                      \\
		\lstset{language=VHDL}
		Partial interface abstraction via \lstinline|component| definitions                                  &
		No interface abstraction                                                                                                                 \\
		Packaging via actual packages                                                                        &
		Packaging via include files                                                                                                              \\
		Useful for high-level modeling                                                                       &
		Better suited for low-level modeling                                                                                                     \\
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\begin{table}[H]
	\center \rowcolors{2}{white}{black!10}
	\begin{tabular} { p{7cm} | p{7cm} }
		More verbose, meant to act both as design and documentation           &
		Less verbose, no packaging capability, harder to extend                                                   \\
		Unambiguous semantics and easily portable desings, no race conditions &
		Ambiguous emulation semantics, race conditions possible, different vendors might have different semantics \\
		No simulation control defined in language                             &
		Basic simulation control defined in language
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Synthesis}

Today, HDLs are increasingly used for designing ICs in a \textit{top-down} approach involving \textbf{synthesis}:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item We \textit{design} the circuit through high-level descriptions;
	\item We leave the conversion to a low-level representation to \textit{synthesis} tools, which automatically convert HDL behavior to HDL circuits;
	\item The last step is, of course, \textit{simulation} to validate the design against the original high-level description.
\end{enumerate}

Synthesis is not the objective of this course.
On the other hand, the use of HDLs for synthesis is growing as circuits get more complex, and synthesis tools are maturing.
One thing to note is that HDLs were originally defined for simulation: some features of the language are not supported by synthesizers (for instance, timing constants don't necessarily translate to low-level components, neither do branching loops such as the \lstinline|while| loop offered by HDLs).

\subsubsection{The Y-chart}

\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{../figures/ychart.png}
\end{center}

There are different ways of modeling a given system, each with different levels of abstraction:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Functional}: what the system \textit{does}: this goes all the way from algorithmic (behavioral) representation to RT languages, boolean functions, up to the \textit{differential equations} which govern the electronic components that make up the system;
	\item \textbf{Structural}: what the system is \textit{made up} of: we can start from high-level blocks such as \textit{processors} and \textit{memories}, look at components which operate at RT level, up to single logic \text{gates} and their implementations based on transistors;
	\item \textbf{Geometric}: how the components of the system are \textit{laid out} physically. This won't be of much interest in this course, as the focus is on \textit{logical} modeling.
\end{itemize}

This relates to a set of ways we can model our systems:
\begin{itemize}
	\item At the \textit{system} level, we see systems as a set of interconnected high-level components and their interfaces (black boxes);
	\item At an \textit{algorithmic} level, we see systems through the algorithms they implement;
	\item At the \textit{RT} level, we see systems through registers and sequential networks that propagate values across them;
	\item At the \textit{logic} level, we see systems through combinatorial networks (made up of gates) implementing boolean functions;
	\item At the \textit{transistor} level, we see how gates themselves are implemented through transistors;
	\item At the \textit{geometry/topology} level, we see how physical components (transistors) are laid out on boards.
\end{itemize}

The data type we use today in VHDL modeling is the \lstinline|std_logic| type, which implements 9-value logic (compared to other versions, which use simple 2-value logic \lstinline|bit| values).

